WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:06.000
Tarun Verma. Four projects on what conservation records leave out, and free tools so that more people can check the evidence. Conservation Science Researcher, India.

00:06.200 --> 00:16.000
Where the question began: Ranthambhore, 2021. When recorded offences fell by more than half. For my M.Sc., I read 1,341 enforcement records from Ranthambhore Tiger Reserve. Recorded offences fell from 225 in 2019 to 98 in 2020. The reading was not that offending had stopped, but that cases were no longer being registered once COVID-19 arrived.

00:16.200 --> 00:28.000
01 of 04. UNEP Tide Turners Plastic Challenge, impact evaluation, 2024 to 2025. A youth programme on plastic that works like a game, from Entry to Leader to Champion. Delivered in India by WWF-India and the Centre for Environment Education, and evaluated by the University of Oxford and ATREE. 664 students in the trial, 55% ahead of non-participants on household recycling, 56 interviews.

00:28.000 --> 00:36.000
What the platform could not show: in Barmer, a teacher climbed a tree in her school yard to find enough phone signal to register her students. Why it matters: it showed where the programme worked and who it could not reach.

00:36.200 --> 00:54.000
02 of 04. WildTrace, an open atlas of illegal wildlife trade in animals and plants. It answers questions from public reports and government trade records, and every answer says who answers it and what its number cannot tell you. Over 1,800 graded cases as of September 2026; in a blind audit, 96.5% were real enforcement events. Why it matters: journalists, researchers and small NGOs can follow the trade and check how strong the evidence is before they repeat a claim.

00:54.200 --> 01:12.000
03 of 04. Protected Area Update, with Kalpavriksh, 2022 to 2024. The newsletter has reported from India's parks since the 1990s. At IIT Bombay I built its geospatial database and sketched how the website's map should work. Over 6,000 reports, more than 1,000 coded by me. Why it matters: three decades of local conservation news are now searchable and mapped for anyone.

01:12.200 --> 01:28.000
04 of 04. Sift QDA, a free qualitative analysis app for Windows. It codes interviews, PDFs and surveys, searches them by meaning, and works with NVivo, ATLAS.ti and MAXQDA projects. Why it matters: students and NGOs can do careful research without a licence, and sensitive interviews never leave their machine.

01:28.200 --> 01:40.500
Open, citable, free. Tarun Verma. So that the people a record leaves out are counted too. tarunverma.com
